The viable route is now clear and it is not the initially suggested classical planted labeling. That construction would be too easy whenever the paper’s greedy palette permits distinct even labels. I’m instead using the paper’s native \(S=\varnothing\) oriented-labeling variant from Section 3, which is exactly a coloring constraint: generate a balanced planted sparse 4-color instance at the Kesten–Stigum boundary, randomly orient every edge, and return the labeling as color classes. This is Track A, with the average-case claim stated cautiously and backed by capped DSATUR plus a spectral recovery attack—not by worst-case NP-hardness alone.
