    10	| intended intuition | constraint propagation through overlapping neighborhoods |
    11	| domain essentiality | native |
    12	| reduction | none |
    13	
    14	This module turns Colucci and Győri's [*On L(2,1)-labelings of oriented
    15	graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.05467) into an exact witness problem.  A
    16	solver receives an oriented simple graph and must assign every vertex one of a
    17	fixed set of even integer labels.  For the paper's `S = empty` variant there is
    18	no two-step-path condition; an arc only requires its endpoint labels to differ
    19	by at least two.  The checker scans the label vector and every arc using integer
    20	comparisons.  It never reads the planted answer.
    21	
    22	**Trust status:** every local mandatory gate passes, including six attacks on
    23	eight shipping seeds.  The directory is nevertheless **not submission-ready**:
    24	the required bare and G9 oracle runs reached OpenRouter, but all twelve redraws
    25	returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded`.  There are zero completed oracle
    26	attempts, so these errors are not counted as evidence that a model failed.
    27	
    28	## Family and Track-A basis
    29	
    30	Section 3 defines the three oriented two-path types `P1`, `P2`, and `P3`, then
    31	defines `L_S` labelings and explicitly identifies the `S = empty` case with
    32	ordinary graph coloring (up to the paper's span convention).  Generation first
    33	draws a shuffled balanced partition and then samples only cross-class edges;
    34	each edge is oriented independently.  The certificate is therefore known by
    35	inverse generation before the public graph exists.
    36	
    37	The shipping distribution has `q=5`, `n=240`, and expected degree 14.  In the
    38	balanced planted-coloring/SBM notation its within-class rate is `a=0` and its

